WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
ALICIA WILLIAMS:
These days, it's

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.400
not enough for your
applications to be sometimes on.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.220
They need a database that
maintains high availability

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.460
while handling increasing
and seasonal loads.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.670
In today's episode of
Spanner Database Unlimited,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.580
we'll show you under the hood
how Cloud Spanner provides

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.510
five nines.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:24.480
That's 99.999% uptime for your
mission critical applications,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.712
even at Google scale.

00:00:26.712 --> 00:00:29.484
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.760
Your customers
demand 24/7 access.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.040
Every second your
data is unavailable,

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.360
you're losing money.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.600
You're not able to process
orders, customers can't log in,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.590
or perhaps your operations are
ground to a halt. Regulations,

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.190
such as ISO 27001
and HIPAA, require

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.920
availability guarantees.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.050
For critical infrastructure
like health care

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.240
or financial
services, downtime can

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.510
have rippling effects into
the economy or public health.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.590
So what are typical causes
of downtime for a database?

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.550
First, the
infrastructure can fail.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.710
Disks die, routers melt
down, or memory is corrupted.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.140
Spanner, like other
distributed systems,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.720
handles these types of
failure by maintaining

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.890
separate copies of your data
across isolated infrastructure.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.530
The more copies
and isolation, the

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.040
lower the probability
of a complete failure.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.490
Let's dig into how
replication works in Spanner.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.920
A regional instance
is composed of three

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.530
identical read-write replicas.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.660
When you provision
one Spanner node,

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.120
you actually get
three units of storage

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.620
and compute that work together.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.310
Each replica contains a
full copy of the database

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.330
and is hosted in a different
physical location or zone.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.940
The zones are organized
to reduce the chances

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.340
that more than one will
fail at the same time,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.180
for example, by having
independent power and cooling.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.260
As data changes, Spanner
updates each of the replicas.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.620
It uses the Paxos consensus
protocol and true time

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.009
to ensure these updates
are strongly consistent.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.600
We'll dive into
Spanner's industry

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.270
leading consistency
guarantees in the next video.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.750
If one zone becomes unavailable,
Spanner automatically

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.330
routes queries to
the other zones.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.200
This all happens online, meaning
in-flight queries won't fail

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.930
and the database can continue
to handle queries and updates.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.870
Once the zone comes
back up, it will

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.750
catch up from the other
replicas automatically

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.930
and resume handling
traffic once it's current.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:42.370
No manual intervention is needed
and there's never any downtime.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.640
Regional Spanner configuration
support up to four nines,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:51.600
or 99.99% availability for
mission critical applications.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.880
While very unlikely,
a region can

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.160
be a single point of
failure, for example,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.750
in a natural disaster.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.890
So Spanner also offers
multi-region configurations.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.710
In this case, it
spreads data over

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.110
geographically dispersed
locations using

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.180
Google's dedicated network.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.480
By spreading replicas over
multiple independent regions,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.940
a multi-region
configuration can achieve up

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.400
to five nines of availability.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.800
For the most demanding
uptime requirements,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.730
such as in financial services,
online gaming and retail,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.330
this is the level of
availability you need.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.970
Spanner does a lot of
work behind the scenes

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.600
to ensure availability,
consistency, and performance

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.160
at scale.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.090
Google's own applications
depend on it today.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.330
However, to your developers,
it looks like a single scale

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.690
up database.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.690
They don't have to worry
about brittle sharding,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.110
eventual consistency,
or manual failover,

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.190
and can focus on innovation.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.010
Another source of
potential downtime

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.600
is regular maintenance.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.610
Software gets
updated, firmware is

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:01.360
patched, security policies are
revised, which in most cases

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.510
means bringing
down the database.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.140
In a world of always-on access
and increasing interdependence

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.260
of business processes,
finding maintenance windows

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.650
across an application
set can be impossible.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.200
With Spanner, though,
planned database maintenance

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.029
is a thing of the past.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.040
Spanner is fully managed.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.650
Google's best in class site
reliability engineering

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:26.530
ensures it's always up
to date, without having

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:28.960
to bring down the database.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.090
A third source of
potential downtime

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.660
is changes in applications
that rely on the database.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.970
If your application
requires a schema change,

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.060
like adding a new table
or changing a data type,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.920
Spanner's unique
schema versioning

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.530
allows you to apply
changes to a live instance

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.930
without missing a beat.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.240
Some schema updates are
applied immediately,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.060
such as adding a
NOT NULL column.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.510
Others require a validation
to avoid violating consistency

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:57.910
constraints or
require backfilling,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.320
such as adding an index.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.390
While this backfilling
happens automatically

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.430
in the background,
Spanner continues

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.540
to service queries against the
previous version of the schema.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.200
Once the changes are completed,
it automatically switches over

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:15.290
to the new schema, providing
a 100% consistent view

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.800
of the data without
incurring any downtime.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.030
Another application change
that can cause downtime, bugs--

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.880
for example, a delete that
leaves off aware condition

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.300
and deletes too much data,
or a calculation error that

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.250
corrupts data.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.680
But Spanner provides
transactionally consistent

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.270
backups, allowing you to roll
back to a known good state.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.790
This is possible because
every update in Spanner

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.590
happens at a time stamp.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.720
Instead of changing
the data in place,

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.910
Spanner marks the
old data as expired

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.480
and new data is
available at this time.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.960
Using this precise change
tracking, backups always

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.510
capture a consistent
snapshot of the data,

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.760
even as data is
updated during the time

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.250
it takes for the
backup to complete.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.890
Restoring a consistent view
of the data from a backup

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.290
is simple and can be
online in minutes,

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:10.440
reducing the recovery time
objective during a disaster.

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.260
Today, we showed you how Spanner
provides availability at scale.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.240
In the next episode,
we're diving

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.370
into Spanner's
consistency at scale.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.850
If you thought you could
only have two of the three,

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:26.126
well, stay tuned for next
episode's conclusion.

00:06:26.126 --> 00:06:26.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.720
Thanks for joining us today
on Spanner Database Unlimited.

